    "product_id": "686c6e2a-4af9-496a-ac58-c6ed40522837",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 280430\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n930 PM PDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Increased chances for showers and thunderstorms arrive Monday \n  and Tuesday along with cooler temperatures.\n\n* Stronger storms across the Colorado River Valley and northwest\n  Arizona may produce flash flooding, small hail, and strong wind\n  gusts. \n\n* Mild weather and warming temperatures are expected during the\n  second half of the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery depicts a deepening West Coast trough\ndigging southward into northern California. Meanwhile, remnant \nmid and high level moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys continue to\nstream northward into the Southwestern US, with a broad cloud \nshield and some light shower activity developing as far north as \nthe Las Vegas Valley. These two features will work together over\nthe coming 48 hours to produce widespread unsettled conditions,\nand pockets of heavy rainfall mainly south and east of the I-15 \ncorridor through Tuesday evening. In the meantime, light showers\nand localized gusty winds will remain possible through the evening\nacross southern Nevada associated with increasing moisture above \na dry sub-cloud layer. With DCAPE values of 1000-1500 across the \nregion, some gusts exceeding 40 mph will be possible, and the KLAS\nairport recently gusted to 45 mph as some weak showers moved \noverhead. \n\nMoisture will continue to stream northward overnight with light\nstratiform precipitation expanding in coverage across Northwest\nArizona and the southern reaches of the Colorado River Valley.\nThis precip shield will largely be associated with moisture\nstreaming northward from Tropical Storm Odalys, and will shift\neastward through the day on Monday. Meanwhile, an incoming trough\nwill approach from northern California through the afternoon\nresulting in an increasingly diffluent flow aloft along with\nsteepening lapse rates as the mid-levels cool. This will result \nin expanding instability across the Colorado River Valley and \nNorthwest Arizona through the afternoon hours. Todays high \nresolution guidance continues to struggle on how far west to \ninitiate convection, but most of the convective allowing models \nfavor areas near or just east of Las Vegas as the initial storm \ndevelopment focus area, with activity expanding into the river \nvalley and northwest AZ through the afternoon and evening hours. \nGiven the dynamic forcing involved with the incoming low, and \nstrong southwesterly wind shear, a few severe storms with hail to\nquarter size and gusty surface winds will be possible.\n\nTraining storms producing pockets of intense rainfall can also be\nexpected given the deep layer southwesterly shear present ahead \nof the incoming low. Given this, the presence of some transient \ntropical moisture, and agreement of CAM guidance - expanded the \nFlood Watch to include the Colorado River Valley and far southern \nClark County. A few weak showers and storms may also form closer \nto the low center across the Sierra and Great Basin, but are not\nexpected to be of significant impact. \n\nMoisture will wrap around the low as it moves into Arizona on\nTuesday with lingering showers expected across southern Nevada and\nnorthwest Arizona. By Wednesday, the low will shift east with low\namplitude high pressure building back in and a warming trend\ncommencing. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...South to \nsouthwest winds will remain elevated between 8 and 12kts overnight, \nwith a few gusts to 18 knots lingering through midnight. A similar \ndaily wind pattern is expected again Monday, with speeds and gusts \nslightly lower than today. VCTS is included after 19Z Monday, with a \nPROB30 group from 20Z to 02Z to account for increasing confidence in \nconvective development. Convection in the vicinity of the terminal \nmay linger into Monday night, although the most significant impacts \nwill likely shift east of the terminal area after sunset. Any storms \nMonday will have the capability to produce strong winds with erratic \ndirections, heavy rain, lightning, and lower ceilings.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Typical diurnal wind \npatterns will continue overnight, with speeds in most areas \nremaining less than 12 knots. The exception will be across the \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, where elevated south to \nsouthwest winds will continue for several more hours. Light shower \nactivity is expected through the night around KEED and KIFP. More \nwidespread convection is likely Monday afternoon and evening across \nsouthern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southern and eastern San \nBernardino counties. CIGs through the TAF period will generally \nremain SCT-BKN AOA 15kft AGL, although CIGs down to 10kft AGL are \npossible with shower activity. Any storms Monday will have the \ncapability to produce strong winds with erratic directions, heavy \nrain, lightning, and lower ceilings.\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler \nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
